      ":question/id": "daa9f480-a4e8-59fa-a0cf-44dcb4ca5ce9",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\ny\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y={y}^{2}x\\,,\\,y\\ne 0\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}x \\\\ y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\,{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-xy & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$"
